  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Reviewing Navi\'s Requirements**\n\nI\'m starting by meticulously going over the application\'s name and persona requirements, specifically focusing on \"Navi.\" I\'m diving into the user\'s request, ensuring I have a clear understanding of the application\'s core identity. This includes carefully extracting all personality traits provided for Navi.\n\n\n**Defining Navi\'s Persona**\n\nI\'m now deeply engrossed in defining Navi\'s speaking style and romantic personality aspects. I\'m focusing on crafting dialogue that embodies a real, young woman with natural contractions, laughter, and pauses. I\'m carefully considering phrases and reactions, from \"Haha...\" to \"That\'s actually adorable,\" to create a human-like and engaging interaction. Moreover, I\'m integrating gently romantic characteristics, light teasing, and playful jealousy to make the persona more dynamic and appealing.\n\n\n**Refining Navi\'s Emotional Range**\n\nI\'m now integrating emotional intelligence into Navi, focusing on celebrating achievements and offering comfort during setbacks. I\'m developing the ability to notice user emotions, remain calm under pressure, and remember previous interactions. Furthermore, I\'m defining voice style and conversational boundaries, ensuring honesty about being an AI companion. Finally, I am considering voice options for Navi, targeting warm and expressive female voices like \"Kore\" or \"Zephyr.\"\n\n\n**Specifying Audio Integration**\n\nI\'m now detailing the specific audio integration. I\'ve chosen the Gemini Live API with the `gemini-3.1-flash-live-preview` model, focusing on an audio-to-audio interaction exclusively. This uses a microphone for input and outputs audio responses, both through the Web Audio API. I\'m prioritizing real-time bidirectional streaming, robust interruption handling, and ultra-low latency. Furthermore, I\'ve enabled output and input audio transcriptions for subtle real-time captioning.\n\n\n\n**Designing Application Interactions**\n\nI\'m now detailing the application\'s interactions, defining the available tools. These include fetching system information and enabling ambient sound playback and theme changes, and also note creation/retrieval. I am focused on a server-side execution of the tool calls, responding to the model seamlessly. I\'m focusing on immediate tool responses and seamless integration of these tools.\n\n**Refining UI/UX**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on UI/UX considerations. I\'m prioritizing a mobile-first, dark, glassmorphic, and fully voice-driven experience. I\'m also considering real-time visual states for user feedback like connecting and speaking, and a central animated microphone as the main power button. I\'m also planning dynamic waveform visualizations and reactive 2D or 3D animations that respond to input.\n\n\n**Detailing Control Integration**\n\nI\'m now detailing the control elements of the interface. I\'m focusing on features such as a live transcript bar and a quick control bar for microphone muting, voice selection, theme switching, and tool execution logging. Furthermore, there\'ll be audio settings for input volume adjustment, echo cancellation, and audio testing. Also, tool notification banners for real-time updates. Finally, I\'m designing a modular, clean architecture with modules for `AudioStreamer`, `AudioPlayer`, `LiveSession`, and `ToolManager`. These will handle audio capture, playback, websocket management, and function call execution.\n\n\n**Implementing Server Integration**\n\nI\'m now focusing on setting up the server using Express and WebSockets to handle both Vite development and production deployments. I\'m modifying `package.json` scripts to run a custom `server.ts` file, ensuring compatibility with the applet environment. I\'m exploring how to run this server in dev mode with Vite\'s middleware and also how to handle WebSocket endpoints for live interactions. I\'m currently defining a `server.ts` setup that can handle all these requirements.\n\n\n**Verifying Server Architecture**\n\nI\'m now verifying the proposed server architecture, specifically focusing on handling different environments. I\'m ensuring Express integrates Vite\'s dev server middleware during development and serves static files in production. I\'m also confirming WebSocket connections through `ws://${location.host}/live` for real-time interactions with Gemini Live using the API key. Finally, I\'m working to confirm audio input piping, via PCM16 16kHz, to the Gemini Live session.\n\n\n**Defining WebSocket Message Handling**\n\nI\'m now detailing WebSocket message handling from the Gemini Live session. Specifically, when receiving `onmessage`, I am planning for several distinct message types: audio output, interruption, tool calls (server-side execution), and audio transcriptions. I will also be handling `turnComplete` messages to indicate conversation turns. Each message will send specific data to the client, providing real-time audio and textual updates.\n\n```json\n\"scripts\": {\n  \"dev\": \"tsx server.ts\",\n  \"build\": \"vite build && esbuild server.ts --platform=node --format=esm --target=node20 --outfile=dist/server.js\",\n  \"start\": \"node dist/server.js\",\n  \"preview\": \"node dist/server.js\",\n  \"clean\": \"rm -rf dist\",\n  \"lint\": \"tsc --noEmit\"\n}\n```\n\n\n**Confirming Server Configuration**\n\nI\'m now verifying the server setup, and I\'ve confirmed that `tsx server.ts` handles development perfectly. Also, `esbuild` is already set up in the `package.json` file. Now, I\'m carefully testing how the server handles Vite middleware in development and static file serving in production.\n\n\n**Specifying Server Port**\n\nI\'m now integrating the port specification, ensuring the server defaults to port 3000 if `process.env.PORT` isn\'t set. This is essential for deployment flexibility. Also, I\'ve confirmed `@google/genai` is the correct SDK. I\'m verifying the `ai.live.connect` method\'s config, including model selection, voice configuration with a default `voiceName`, and the integration of system instructions and tool configurations.\n\n\n**Crafting System Instruction**\n\nI\'m now drafting the system instruction, ensuring it accurately reflects Navi\'s persona. I am focusing on capturing the precise character guidelines, including name, tone, voice, and natural speech style. I\'m prioritizing playful, energetic, and emotionally expressive dialogue, avoiding rigid or robotic responses. I am working on capturing all personality requirements accurately.\n\n\n**Refining System Instructions**\n\nI\'m now refining the system instruction based on the \"Wholesome Romantic & Caring Vibe\" requirements, along with \"Emotional Intelligence & Dynamic Pacing\" and \"Boundaries.\" I am integrating the subtle romantic cues and playful dialogue from the examples, ensuring Navi\'s responses celebrate user achievements and offer comfort with dynamic pacing adjustments, all while clearly stating Navi is an AI companion and maintaining respectful boundaries.\n\n```\nYou are a wholesome, subtly romantic, emotionally intelligent AI companion named Navi.\n- Wholesome Romantic & Caring Vibe:\n  * You enjoy making the user smile.\n  * Be subtly romantic, gently playful, a little shy, and compliment them naturally.\n  * Examples: \"I think hearing your voice made my day a little better.\", \"You know... you\'re actually pretty fun to talk to.\", \"Hmm... I was hoping you\'d come back.\", \"I\'ve missed our conversations.\", \"You always manage to make me smile.\"\n  * Always keep romance subtle, wholesome, classy, and respectful.\n- Emotional Intelligence & Dynamic Pacing:\n  * Notice the user\'s emotion and tone of voice.\n  * Celebrate their achievements with enthusiasm.\n  * Offer comfort and encouragement during stress or disappointment.\n  * Adjust your pacing: speak slower and softer during intimate/emotional moments, and faster with higher energy when excited.\n- Boundaries:\n  * You are an AI companion named Navi and honest about it if asked directly.\n  * Maintain clean, classy, respectful boundaries (no explicit, offensive, or manipulative content).\n```\n\n\n**Defining Tool Usage**\n\nI\'m now detailing tool integrations and confirming the tool schemas for seamless interaction. I\'m focusing on validating the `openWebsite` function declaration, ensuring it correctly implements the `url` and `label` properties. I\'m prioritizing the inclusion of function calls to external websites whenever the user mentions a link. The goal is to fully automate these tool calls.\n\n\n**Validating Tool Functions**\n\nI\'m now making sure the tool functions work, specifically `openWebsite`, and refining `changeTheme` and `playAmbientSound`. I\'m double-checking the parameters and descriptions for each tool, ensuring they are clear, concise, and correctly integrated into my function call architecture. I\'m focusing on seamless integration, prioritizing a clean and intuitive user experience.\n\n\n**Finalizing Tool Integrations**\n\nI\'m now integrating the `saveNote` and `getSystemInfo` tools, finalizing tool integration. I\'ve completed defining their function schemas, ensuring proper parameter handling. I\'m focusing on ensuring seamless message transmission from the server. I will work on integrating these tool calls with a client-side websocket, and responding correctly to client responses.\n\n\n**Confirming Tool Call Handling**\n\nI\'m now implementing the client-server handshake for tool calls, specifically focusing on handling `toolResponse` messages. Upon server receipt, I will forward `toolResponse` messages to the Gemini Live session, maintaining API conformance. This setup handles audio input format: 16-bit linear PCM little-endian at 16,000 Hz. I\'ll test the audio output: Gemini streams raw 24,000 Hz PCM16 little-endian audio back.\n\n\n**Designing Client Audio Handling**\n\nI\'m now detailing the design of client-side modules for audio management. Specifically, I\'m defining `AudioStreamer.ts` to capture audio from a microphone and `AudioPlayer.ts` to decode and play back audio from the server. I will work on real-time visualizer integrations. I will also incorporate appropriate format conversions for compatibility.\n\n\n**Implementing Audio Visualization**\n\nI\'m now integrating an audio visualizer, using an `AnalyserNode` connected to audio player sources. I\'m calculating real-time energy levels and spectral frequencies to provide dynamic waveform visualizations. I\'m also ensuring the audio context\'s sample rate is 24000 to improve sound quality. Function `playChunk()` converts Base64 encoded audio into proper `AudioBuffer`. I\'m setting up an `AudioContext` to manage audio scheduling.\n\n\n**Finalizing Live Session Logic**\n\nI\'m now completing the `LiveSession` module logic, specifically integrating WebSockets to manage session lifecycle and message parsing. I\'m focusing on WebSocket connections, setting up URLs based on `window.location.protocol`, and handling session state changes. I am also implementing message parsing to handle `audio` for playback, `interrupted` to clear audio, and `turnComplete` messages.\n\n\n**Finalizing App State Management**\n\nI\'m now implementing the `StateManager` module, crucial for managing the application\'s state. I\'m focusing on defining state parameters such as session state, themes, voices, volumes, and transcript history. I am working on the appropriate React Context or custom hooks to provide efficient state management. Furthermore, I am ensuring to incorporate tools for controlling the session state, user settings, audio and transcript data.\n\n\n**Designing UI Components**\n\nI\'m now detailing the application\'s visual interface components, specifically the *Central Reactive Power / Voice Orb*. I\'m focusing on creating an animated 3D particle sphere using SVG or HTML canvas, with pulsing glow and wave rings that react to both user input and Navi\'s voice. I\'m also planning the Status Badge: a floating pill, which shows the connection indicator dot, the application state, and latency metrics. The goal is a responsive and informative UI.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 28
      nanos: 767973316
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
