  user_input {
    user_response: "Remove the embedded browser completely.\n\nDo NOT render websites inside the Navi UI.\n\nDo NOT use an iframe, BrowserView, WebView, embedded Chromium, or integrated browser panel.\n\nInstead, Navi should control the user\'s installed Google Chrome.\n\nBehavior:\n\n\342\200\242 If Chrome is not running:\n  - Launch Google Chrome.\n  - Open the requested website.\n\n\342\200\242 If Chrome is already running:\n  - Bring the existing Chrome window to the foreground.\n  - Reuse the current Chrome session.\n  - Open a new tab only when necessary.\n\nVoice examples:\n\n\"Play music\"\n\342\206\222 Bring Chrome to the front.\n\342\206\222 Open YouTube Music (or Spotify Web).\n\n\"Open YouTube\"\n\342\206\222 Activate Chrome.\n\342\206\222 Open youtube.com.\n\n\"Search Naruto\"\n\342\206\222 Activate Chrome.\n\342\206\222 Search Google for \"Naruto\".\n\n\"Gmail\"\n\342\206\222 Activate Chrome.\n\342\206\222 Open Gmail.\n\nDo not duplicate Chrome windows unnecessarily.\n\nNavi should only display a small floating companion widget.\n\nThe floating widget should contain:\n\n- Current action\n- Voice waveform\n- Listening/Speaking indicator\n- Pause button\n- Stop button\n- Mic button\n- Current media title (if music is playing)\n\nThe widget should always stay on top.\n\nIt should be draggable.\n\nIt should occupy less than 20% of the screen.\n\nIt should never display webpages.\n\nThe browser experience must always happen in the user\'s real Google Chrome application.\n\nThe embedded browser UI must be removed entirely.\n\nAll browser-related voice commands should execute through tool calls that interact with the system Chrome instead of an internal browser."
    items {
      text: "Remove the embedded browser completely.\n\nDo NOT render websites inside the Navi UI.\n\nDo NOT use an iframe, BrowserView, WebView, embedded Chromium, or integrated browser panel.\n\nInstead, Navi should control the user\'s installed Google Chrome.\n\nBehavior:\n\n\342\200\242 If Chrome is not running:\n  - Launch Google Chrome.\n  - Open the requested website.\n\n\342\200\242 If Chrome is already running:\n  - Bring the existing Chrome window to the foreground.\n  - Reuse the current Chrome session.\n  - Open a new tab only when necessary.\n\nVoice examples:\n\n\"Play music\"\n\342\206\222 Bring Chrome to the front.\n\342\206\222 Open YouTube Music (or Spotify Web).\n\n\"Open YouTube\"\n\342\206\222 Activate Chrome.\n\342\206\222 Open youtube.com.\n\n\"Search Naruto\"\n\342\206\222 Activate Chrome.\n\342\206\222 Search Google for \"Naruto\".\n\n\"Gmail\"\n\342\206\222 Activate Chrome.\n\342\206\222 Open Gmail.\n\nDo not duplicate Chrome windows unnecessarily.\n\nNavi should only display a small floating companion widget.\n\nThe floating widget should contain:\n\n- Current action\n- Voice waveform\n- Listening/Speaking indicator\n- Pause button\n- Stop button\n- Mic button\n- Current media title (if music is playing)\n\nThe widget should always stay on top.\n\nIt should be draggable.\n\nIt should occupy less than 20% of the screen.\n\nIt should never display webpages.\n\nThe browser experience must always happen in the user\'s real Google Chrome application.\n\nThe embedded browser UI must be removed entirely.\n\nAll browser-related voice commands should execute through tool calls that interact with the system Chrome instead of an internal browser."
